# Caiman

**Versioned hardware and specification context for firmware development.**

Caiman helps firmware engineers organize the reference manuals, board details,
and customer requirements that coding agents need. It connects a project to an
explicit board version and a pinned set of documents, so updating a manual does
not silently change an existing configuration.

[Getting started](README.md#first-launch-and-document-ingestion) ·
[Documentation](README.md#documentation) ·
[Roadmap](docs/ROADMAP.md)

## What you can do today

- **Register documents.** Import prepared Markdown unchanged, validate its
  headings or requirement IDs, and assign explicit access labels.
- **Model hardware.** Create and edit boards with parts, document references,
  and declared relationships.
- **Define projects.** Pin a board and specification versions, then declare
  features and document precedence.
- **Preserve exact versions.** Content hashes identify immutable snapshots;
  adopting a changed document or board is an explicit action.
- **Manage repositories.** Validate and register existing Caiman repositories,
  unregister them, or initialize a compartment repository with an optional
  initial metadata push.

**Development status: pre-MVP.** Local ingestion and board/project authoring are
available through a terminal interface and JSON drafts. Session workspace
generation, brief generation, document-access logging, and shared Git storage
are planned. See the [README](README.md) for currently available commands and
integrations.

## Quick start

You will need **Python 3.11 or newer** and an interactive terminal. From the
repository root, run:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/python -m pip install -r requirements-dev.lock
.venv/bin/python -m pip install --no-build-isolation --no-deps -e .
.venv/bin/caiman
```

For your first walkthrough:

1. Create a board and a project from the dashboard. Both can start without
   documents.
2. Register the synthetic manual in `fixtures/reference-manual.md`, following
   the [ingestion guide](README.md#first-launch-and-document-ingestion).
3. Edit the board to select the manual, then explicitly adopt the updated board
   in your project.
4. Review and register each configuration to preserve its exact document pins.

Use `h/j/k/l` to navigate, `Enter` or `i` to edit, `Escape` to leave text editing,
and `q` to go back while navigating.

## Core concepts

| Concept | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **Document** | One unchanged Markdown file with metadata and an explicit access label |
| **Board** | A versioned description of hardware and its supporting documents |
| **Project** | A board version plus specifications, features, and declared precedence |
| **Compartment** | The customer or project access group for a private document |
| **Digest pin** | A content hash identifying an exact immutable version |

Each document is explicitly public or belongs to **exactly one compartment**.
A project can reference documents from several compartments. The planned shared
backend uses one private Git repository per compartment, with the Git host
enforcing teammate access.

Documents are stored locally at `~/.local/share/caiman/store` by default.
Registration does not commit, push, or send document contents to a service.
Keep document stores outside firmware repositories, and use the included
synthetic fixtures when trying the tool or contributing tests.

